//~ compile_fail(backend)
//~ error[KORU030]: Phantom state mismatch

// NEGATIVE (must FAIL to compile): bring an interface up and walk away without
// moving a single frame.
//
// This is pillar 4's asymmetry, and it is the whole reason `carried` is a state.
// `park` accepts ONLY <!carried>, and <carried!> is minted ONLY by `tx`, `rx` or
// the explicit `traffic.skipped`. `start` mints <running!>. So the chain below
// asks `park` to accept a <running!> handle and the compiler refuses.
//
// What the C does instead: nothing at all, and it could not do otherwise.
// `uknetdev` has no teardown call to put a guard on — `exportsyms.uk` is 33
// lines of acquire, configure, query and transfer. A program that probes a NIC,
// negotiates features, allocates a receive ring of `nb_max` buffers, arms two
// virtqueues and then never sends or receives is not a program C has anywhere to
// object to. It is also a real waste: the ring stays allocated for the life of
// the machine, because nothing frees it either.
//
// The legitimate case is still expressible, and loudly: `net:traffic.skipped`
// mints <carried!> without running a C call, so a link check that only wants to
// know the interface comes up says so in a word you can grep for. What this
// rejects is the SILENT no-op. See traffic_skipped_link_check.kz for the same
// chain with the escape in it, compiling clean.

// The `unikraft` namespace is declared here, not inherited: koruc's built-in
// alias probes the sibling koru-libs CHECKOUT, so in a worktree this file would
// otherwise compile against main's lifts instead of the ones beside it.
~std/compiler:paths {
    unikraft: {{ ENTRY }}/../..
}

~import unikraft/net
~import std/io

~unikraft/net:get(id: 0)
| link a |> unikraft/net:probe(a.dev)
    | ok p |> unikraft/net:configure(dev: p)
        | ok c |> unikraft/net:queues.open(c.dev)
            | ok q |> unikraft/net:start(dev: q)
                | ok r |> unikraft/net:park(dev: r)
                | refused e |> unikraft/net:abandon(e.dev)
            | refused e |> unikraft/net:abandon(e.dev)
        | refused e |> unikraft/net:abandon(e.dev)
    | refused e |> unikraft/net:abandon(e.dev)
| missing why |> std/io:print.ln("nic0 unavailable: {{ why:s }}")
